<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Barony of Chalandritsa</h1><p class="st-meta">1209 CE – 1429 CE (approx.) · capital Chalandritsa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Frankish barony in the hills south of Patras, one of the original fiefs of the Principality of Achaea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chalandritsa was among the baronies created in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a> around 1209. It was granted to Robert de la Tremouille, a knight from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and held a small number of knight's fiefs in the hill country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> south of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barony-of-patras">Patras</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The barons owed military service to the prince and had seats in his high court. The Dramelay family later held it for much of the 13th and 14th centuries. Beyond these facts, recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> and Angevin documents, little is known of the barony's internal life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the final collapse of the principality, Chalandritsa was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine Palaiologos</a>, in 1429, at the same time as the campaign that won <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barony-of-patras">Patras</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barony-of-chalandritsa.jpg" alt="Barony of Chalandritsa" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting); derivative work by Constantine · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1209 CE – 1429 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chalandritsa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chalandritsa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1319&amp;focus=barony-of-chalandritsa" aria-label="Barony of Chalandritsa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5223 808 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Barony of Chalandritsa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5543 1064L5530 1071M5543 1064L5560 1098"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5530 1071h0M5560 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5543 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5543 1064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1319&amp;focus=barony-of-chalandritsa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

